          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "Sorry you\u2019re dealing with that. For persistent lower back pain in Singapore, these are reliable places to book, depending on your budget and how fast you want to be seen:\n\nPublic hospitals (subsidised option via polyclinic referral)\n- Singapore General Hospital (SGH) Spine Service, Outram. Large multidisciplinary spine unit. Book after getting a polyclinic/GP referral (for subsidised rates). ([sgh.com.sg](https://www.sgh.com.sg/our-specialties/orthopaedic-surgery/spine?utm_source=openai))\n- National University Hospital (NUH) \u2013 Spine specialists under the National University Spine Institute, Kent Ridge. Book through NUHS or with a referral for subsidised care. ([nuh.com.sg](https://www.nuh.com.sg/care-at-nuh/services/orthopaedic-surgery/spine-specialist?utm_source=openai))\n- Changi General Hospital (CGH) \u2013 CGH\u2013NNI Integrated Spine Centre, Simei/Bedok (East). Good if you\u2019re based in the East. ([cgh.com.sg](https://www.cgh.com.sg/our-specialties/spine-centre?utm_source=openai))\n- Sengkang General Hospital (SKH) \u2013 Spine Hub, Sengkang (Northeast). Triage-and-refer model to get you to the right team quickly. ([skh.com.sg](https://www.skh.com.sg/our-specialties/skh-spine-hub?utm_source=openai))\n- National Neuroscience Institute (NNI) clinics at SGH and Tan Tock Seng Hospital (Novena) \u2013 for nerve-related back issues or if told you may need neurosurgical input; usually requires referral. ([nni.com.sg](https://www.nni.com.sg/clinic-visit/planning-your-clinic-visit?utm_source=openai))\n\nPrivate hospitals/centres (fastest access, higher cost)\n- Mount Elizabeth Medical Centre (Orchard/Novena) \u2013 many orthopaedic spine and neurosurgery clinics; you can request an appointment directly. ([memc.com.sg](https://www.memc.com.sg/make-an-appointment/?utm_source=openai))\n- Raffles Hospital \u2013 Raffles Pain Management (for non-surgical pain procedures) with onward referral as needed. ([rafflesmedicalgroup.com](https://www.rafflesmedicalgroup.com/clinic/raffles-pain-management/?utm_source=openai))\n\nHow to choose\n- If you want the most cost-effective path: visit your nearest polyclinic or a CHAS GP and ask for a referral to an orthopaedic spine clinic (you\u2019ll get subsidised rates at public hospitals). ([moh.gov.sg](https://www.moh.gov.sg/seeking-healthcare/find-a-facility-or-service/types-of-medical-facilities-and-services/primary-care-services/?utm_source=openai))\n- If you need to be seen quickly and are okay with private fees or have insurance: call Mount Elizabeth Medical Centre and ask for the earliest spine specialist slot, or book at Raffles. ([memc.com.sg](https://www.memc.com.sg/make-an-appointment/?utm_source=openai))\n\nWhat to do now\n- Book an initial assessment (public via polyclinic referral; private directly) and bring any prior scans, a list of symptoms, what worsens/relieves the pain, and medications tried. For most persistent low back pain, first-line management is non\u2011surgical (activity modification, physiotherapy, and targeted meds), with imaging or injections only if indicated by the specialist. SGH/CGH/SKH/NUH all provide these services within their spine pathways. ([sgh.com.sg](https://www.sgh.com.sg/our-specialties/orthopaedic-surgery/spine?utm_source=openai))\n\nUrgent red flags (go to the nearest A&E or call 995 now)\n- New or worsening leg weakness, numbness around the groin/saddle area, loss of bladder/bowel control, fever with back pain, major trauma, or cancer/IV drug use with severe back pain.\n\nIf you share your area (e.g., East, West, Northeast) and preference (public vs private, budget/insurance), I can point you to the most convenient clinic and the fastest way to book."
        }
